Subject: Expose where a linearised rope keeps its flat string

What this does
  - The layout header gains the offsets of a rope's `left` and `right` values. A rope the
    engine has linearised once holds the flat string on the left and the empty string on the
    right (`js_linearize_string_rope` memoises it), so a unit can read the flat string and
    index it without a call.

Why we need it
  A string built with `s += ...` past the flat-concatenation limit is a rope. The compiled
  `s.charCodeAt(i)`, `s[i]` and `s.length` declined a rope, left to the generic body on every
  iteration, and a loop over such a string ran entirely outside the speculative body once the
  re-entry limit was spent (hand_005: 1.17x). With the memoised form read in place, the first
  call linearises it (the engine's own path) and every later one stays in compiled code.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -60919,6 +60919,8 @@
     out->class_c_function = JS_CLASS_C_FUNCTION;
     out->off_cfunc_c_function = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSObject, u.cfunc.c_function);
     out->off_cfunc_magic = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSObject, u.cfunc.magic);
+    out->off_rope_left = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSStringRope, left);
+    out->off_rope_right = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSStringRope, right);
     out->off_regpic_misses = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSRegPIC, misses);
     out->reg_pic_add_w = REG_PIC_ADD_W;
     out->off_rt_ic_epoch = (uint32_t)offsetof(JSRuntime, ic_watchpoint_epoch);
--- a/quickjs-aot-rt.h
+++ b/quickjs-aot-rt.h
@@ -170,6 +170,9 @@
     uint32_t off_regpic_misses, reg_pic_add_w, off_rt_ic_epoch;
     uint32_t off_object_flags16, object_extensible_mask, object_noadd_mask;
     uint32_t object_inline_cap_mask, object_inline_cap_shift;
+    /* A rope whose linearised form is memoised holds the flat string on the left and the
+       empty string on the right: where those two values sit. */
+    uint32_t off_rope_left, off_rope_right;
 } JSAotGenLayout;
 
